Thomas Bates (bates@pvax2.dist.maricopa.edu) wrote:
: In article <55ahs3$138@tom.amherst.edu>, pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu (Paul D. Chapin) writes:
: >The comsat processes probably indicate that the system is confused
: >about whether pfeifer is connected or not.  I've had a similar problem
: >with users not being on, but being reported as connected by "who" and
: >the like.  The result is that biff, which is what comsat really is,
: >get's fired up but doesn't have anywhere to send the notice.  I'm
: >currently talking to DEC support about this.

: I'm having this exact same problem, after a recent upgrade from 3.2G to
: 4.0A. 

I should add that there's a real security problem with this.
Apparently biff looks up what terminal line a user is one and sends
the notice down that line.  If the person isn't really on, you get
the comsat that wouldn't go away.  The bigger danger is that somebody
else is now sitting at the end of the line.  I've had reports of the
wrong person getting biff notices, including the first seven lines or
so of the message.

