On 18 Nov 1996 23:37:31 GMT, Brian S Hiles <bsh20858@news.fhda.edu> wrote:
>Craig Martin wrote:
>: HELP!!! I currently work at an ISP in Toronto, Ontario, Canada and we
>: are having problems with the number of processes we can run our Dec
>: Alpha Server 1000. We have 128 megs of RAM and yet we can't seem to be
>: able to run more than 130 processes. Anymore than that and we crash. We
>: could run 130 processes on 64 megs of RAM but since the installation of
>: another 64 megs we can't seem to get more processes running. Why?
>: Shouldn't we be able to get many more than 130. If ANYONE has ANY
>: information about this please let me know.
>
>Sounds like a job for customer support.

	Sounds more like a tuning issue.

	For the purpose of the following, I'm assuming you're running DEC
	UNIX (either 3.2+ or 4.0+)...

	In the /sys/conf directory, look for the configuration file used
	to gen your system (there'll be a GENERIC configuration;  If you
	need to, make a copy of it as all CAPS, normally named after the
	node you're configuring).

	Once you've got the configuration file, start looking for the
	sizing tags...

		maxusers	32

	You'll want to tune the "maxusers" tag to match what you're looking
	for.  Remember, please, "maxusers" really sets the number of
	processes and the number of buffers and the number of open files
	all at once, so this is a non-trivial adjustment.  It doesn't
	actually set a limit to the number of users, BTW.

	There is also (I think) licensing of the OS from DEC for the # of
	users that can log in;  My AlphaStation can only have 2 distinct
	login names present on it at a time.  You may need (instead) to
	have the license updated.

	So I don't have all of the answers.  But at least I have some of
	the questions!

