X-Deja-AN: 198249090
references: <328CB3F4.6C45@interware.net> <328D1365.163B@rell.com> <56qrvr$1pe@tiptoe.fhda.edu> <slrn45969vh.52k.campbejr@phu989.um.us.sbphrd.com>
organization: Delft University of Technology, Dept. of Electrical Engineering
newsgroups: comp.unix.admin,comp.unix.aix,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.questions,comp.unix.shell


>	In the /sys/conf directory, look for the configuration file used
>	to gen your system (there'll be a GENERIC configuration;  If you
>	need to, make a copy of it as all CAPS, normally named after the
>	node you're configuring).

>	Once you've got the configuration file, start looking for the
>	sizing tags...

>		maxusers	32

We had a similar problem on our system. It's a DEC Alpha, don't know
the exact type number. Running OSF 3.2.

We were running about 25 http servers (as user 'nobody') and the
server wouldn't execute cgi scripts anymore ('fork' failed).

Solved it by adding the following in /etc/sysconfigtab:

proc:
        max-proc-per-user = 100      

and do a reboot.
Don't know how similar your problem is, but this helped in our case.
Do a man sysconfigtab or man sysconfigdb for more info.

Hop this helps.

Joost Nieuwenhuijse




